;;;; The reproducer for a transpiler stack death. `mystery' answers a
;;;; template instance no declaration opens, so `v' never resolves past
;;;; `auto' -- and the member call then asked deep-typeof for auto's
;;;; type, whose lookup handed the same auto spec straight back:
;;;; (*gets* '|auto|) -> a spec with typeof auto and no default ->
;;;; recurse with identical arguments, no floor, and SBCL died
;;;; "Control stack exhausted" with nothing named.
;;;;
;;;; The guard in authority.lisp answers nil for an auto with nothing
;;;; to resolve through. What follows from nil depends on where the
;;;; access sits: here the member is simply emitted as written and C++
;;;; decides -- the same passthrough an undeclared free name gets, and
;;;; std::vector does have size(), so this file COMPILES. Where the
;;;; resolver genuinely needs the type it refuses instead, naming the
;;;; access ("unknown struct type: auto"). Either is the contract;
;;;; the stack death was the bug. deep-typeof-loop.sh asserts that.
;;;;
;;;; It lives beside its .sh rather than under test/cpp so run.sh's
;;;; scan does not double-count it; the .sh is the test.

(source "guard.cpp"
  (make :cpp #t :compile #t)
  (include <vector>)
  (decl) (func mystery () (out (t<> std::vector int)))
  (func f () (out int)
    (letin* ((v (mystery)))
      (return (cast int (($ v size)))))))
